        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "985a9572-7b7c-517e-a242-b3aa2b46bc86",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: skogsrå, hulder, huldra, rå\n- Topics: Swedish folklore, Scandinavian folklore, Sámi folklore, mythical forest beings\n\nFacts:\n- In Swedish folklore, the skogsrå, also called Forest Rå, Skogsfrun, Skogssnuvan, Skogsnymfen, Råndan, or huldra, was a mythical forest being identified as a rå.\n- In Scandinavian folklore, a hulder is a seductive forest creature.\n- In Swedish folklore, the hulder is known as the skogsrå, meaning “forest spirit,” and as Tallemaja, meaning “pine tree Mary.”\n- In Sámi folklore, the hulder is known as ulda.\n- The hulder’s name suggests that she was originally the same being as the divine figure Huld from völva tradition and the German Holda.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
